or:#333;font:400 18.5px/26px georgia,serif;margin:0 0 10px"><span>By Adam B=
=2E Ellick</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:=
#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>It wasn=E2=80=99t so long ago that the Amazon w=
as a left-wing stronghold. In 2010, the Workers=E2=80=99 Party of President=
 Luiz In=C3=A1cio Lula da Silva won nearly 70 percent of the vote in the ni=
ne states that make up the Brazilian Amazon.</span></p><p style=3D"font:nor=
mal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>This year, in=
 the first round of voting on Oct. 4, Lula won just 46 percent of that vote=
=2E</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;ma=
rgin:0 0 15px"><span>A region once associated with Indigenous tribes and en=
vironmental activists is increasingly dominated by cowboy-chic culture =E2=
=80=94 rodeos draw massive crowds, and country music celebrating ranch life=
 tops the charts. And on Sunday, voters in one of the world=E2=80=99s most =
important environmental regions will head to the polls, with all signs indi=
cating that a majority will vote in favor of Fl=C3=A1vio Bolsonaro, who den=
ies that humans are responsible for climate change.</span></p><p style=3D"f=
ont:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>It=E2=
=80=99s a stunning swing, with potentially enormous consequences beyond Bra=
zil. And what it reveals is that Brazil=E2=80=99s main left-wing party =E2=
=80=94 a party that cares about environmental protection =E2=80=94 has been=
 unable to come up with an approach to saving the Amazon that is also polit=
ically sustainable.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,ser=
if;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>=E2=80=9CFor decades, Brazil has been =
alternating between plundering the Amazon for resources or keeping it bucol=
ic and green,=E2=80=9D Salo Coslovsky, an expert on the Amazonian economy a=
t New York University told me. =E2=80=9CBut neither option produces a stabl=
e outcome.=E2=80=9D</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,ser=
if;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span style=3D"font-weight:700">The =E2=80=
=98What comes next=3F=E2=80=99 problem</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17=
px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>The Amazon is incre=
dibly important for the world. It=E2=80=99s the planet=E2=80=99s largest tr=
opical rainforest. Its trees store well over 100 billion tons of planet-war=
ming carbon, keeping it out of the atmosphere. It has also long been viewed=
 as a potential source of income =E2=80=94 from the rubber boom at the turn=
 of the 20th century to the logging, cattle and mining industries that have=
 recently razed large swaths of its trees.</span></p><p style=3D"font:norma=
l 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Leftist governm=
ents have made huge progress slowing deforestation, which in the last year =
reached a record low, deploying satellites to spot clearings in real time a=
nd sending agents to seize illegal timber and cattle.</span></p><table bord=
