 15px"><span style=3D"font-weight:700">Missed opportunities</span></p><p st=
yle=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><spa=
n>Some economists see missed opportunities. Brazilian companies spend about=
 $800 million a year importing cocoa beans from Ivory Coast and palm oils f=
rom Indonesia and Colombia. These crops grow well in the Amazon, and they c=
ould be farmed on land that was cleared years ago.</span></p><p style=3D"fo=
nt:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Brazil =
also sells raw Amazonian goods like robusta coffee and black pepper to othe=
r countries, which then do the lucrative part =E2=80=94 processing them for=
 markets like Europe.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,s=
erif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>The most egregious example is probab=
ly Brazil=E2=80=99s namesake nut. Some Brazil nuts are harvested in Brazil,=
 but then sold to Bolivia and Peru, which shell and package them. These nut=
s are later exported to Europe =E2=80=94 or sometimes even back to Brazil.<=
/span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin=
:0 0 15px"><span>Brazil=E2=80=99s stock of deforested land in the Amazon is=
 double the size of California, and a lot of it remains idle or underutiliz=
ed. While cattle ranching remains one of the more lucrative uses, experts s=
ay nuts, cacao, exotic fruits and coffee are all part of an untapped forest=
ry system that, paired with forest plantations and more productive cattle r=
anching, could provide more sustainable economic alternatives, if anyone pu=
shed for them.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;co=
lor:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>But the economy of the Amazon, simply put, =
has never been a</span><span style=3D"font-weight:700"> </span><span>priori=
ty for the rest of Brazil, which has competing infrastructure and developme=
nt needs, Ver=C3=ADssimo said. It is often an afterthought for national lea=
ders, many of whom have never visited or don=E2=80=99t grasp its complexity=
 and opportunity.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif=
;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>The Amazon holds at least a third of the=
 world=E2=80=99s remaining tropical forests. Some sources put it at more li=
ke half. Its rains supply farms and cities across South America. That makes=
 the Brazilian presidential election one of the most important in the world=
 for anyone who cares about the planet.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 1=
7px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>The 20 million vot=
ers who live in the Brazilian Amazon will vote in an election in which the =
two candidates were separated in the first round by roughly 2.2 million vot=
es. That no major party in Brazil has yet figured out a sustainable way to =
persuade many of these voters to preserve the environment where they live i=
s now poised to have consequences for everyone.</span></p><table border=3D"=
